content.text:
Fixed. The bug in `src/duration.js` was that the loop `return`ed after the **first** part instead of accumulating all parts, so `1h30m` yielded only `3600000`.

The fix sums each matched part into `total`, then validates finiteness of the final sum before returning:

```js
let total = 0;
const parts = text.matchAll(/(\d+(?:\.\d+)?)(ms|[smhd])/g);
for (const [, amount, unit] of parts) {
  total += Number(amount) * UNIT_MS[unit];
}
if (!Number.isFinite(total)) throw new TypeError('Duration overflow');
return total;
```

The existing full-string regex `^(?:\d+(?:\.\d+)?(?:ms|[smhd]))+$` already guarantees all-or-nothing matching (no partial acceptance of malformed strings, no whitespace between parts), so only the accumulation needed fixing.

Verified with `node --test` (3/3 pass) plus extra edge-case checks: compound/repeated units, any order (`1m1h`), leading/trailing whitespace, zero, fractional ms, `TypeError` for non-strings, empty/whitespace-only strings, embedded whitespace (`1h 30m`), unknown/case-mismatched units (`1H`, `2weeks`), trailing garbage, bare numbers, and non-finite overflow (`9`×400 + `d`).
